## Reduced scan

Without the plugin, the scan uses git and the file list only. It reads no file contents, writes nothing, and prints the report in the chat.

1. Run `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`. If it fails, say this is not a git repository and offer the interview.
2. Run exactly these commands and nothing else:
   - `git ls-files`: the file list. Judge Intent, Build and Gates by file names only.
   - `git rev-parse --abbrev-ref origin/HEAD`: the default branch. If it fails, use `HEAD`.
   - `git log --first-parent --merges --since="90 days ago" --shortstat --format="@%ct %s" <branch>`: merges, their size and subjects. If it prints nothing, run the same without `--merges` and count commits instead.
   - `git log -1 --first-parent --format=%ct <branch>`: the time of the last change.
   - `git for-each-ref --format="%(creatordate:short)" refs/tags`: tags.
   - `git log --since="90 days ago" --format=%ae <branch> | sort -u | wc -l`: the number of contributors, so no address reaches the chat. This is the only pipe allowed.
3. Apply the verdict table. What needs file contents is "nicht geprüft (reduzierter Scan)" (en: "not checked (reduced scan)"): acceptance criteria, the checks CI runs, coverage thresholds, strict typing and agent permissions. Count each of them as missing, and mark the row "(reduzierter Scan)" (en: "(reduced scan)"). Review is at best yellow, and time to first review and review rounds are "n/a: braucht GitHub-Daten" (en: "n/a: needs GitHub data"). The follow-up fix rate is simplified to the share of merges whose subject starts with "fix" or "revert"; say so.
4. Print the report in the chat, in this order, in the user's language:
   1. "Der Stau steckt gerade bei: <stage> (<group>)" (en: "The jam is at: <stage> (<group>)"), and one or two lines why.
   2. A table with the five stages, verdict and evidence: the file names or the git command each rests on.
   3. Up to four numbers (PR size as median changed lines, merges per week, tags in 90 days, follow-up fix rate), each with its source command and count, and the "too few" line below 10 merges.
   4. Three next steps from the step list, starting with the jam stage.
   5. The offer line by the offer table, including what the scan could not judge.
   6. "Zusammenfassung zum Weitergeben" (en: "Summary to share"): one line per stage with verdict and counts, the jam, and the number of contributors. No `/`, no paths below the top level, no names of people, no repository name or URL, no code fence.
5. Ask whether the user wants the email draft. Write it only after a yes, as in "Email draft", with the summary as the body.
